  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "A car travels on a straight road in such a way that its position, in meters, after $t$ seconds is given by the function $f(t).$ The values of $f(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n| **$t$** | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ | $10$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(t)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $14$ | $15$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nApproximate the velocity ${f}^{′}(t)$ at the point $t=4$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=2.$",
  "worked_solution": "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{f(t+h)-f(t-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\nat the time $t=4$ with step size $h=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(4) & ≈\\frac{f(4+2)-f(4-2)}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(6)-f(2)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =0.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the car at the instant of time $t=4$ is $0.75\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$",
